# Selection tiles: the tile is the check

When the user picks **one option out of a few**, and each option has a title (plus an optional
icon and a short description) — export type, print layout, download vs. email — render the
options as **selectable tiles**, not as radio buttons.

The tile **is** the control. Its own border and background show which one is selected; there is
no radio dot (or checkbox) inside it.

## Do

- Use `BillingOptionTile` (`components/billing/billing-action-dialog.tsx`), laid out in a grid:
  `grid gap-1.5 sm:grid-cols-N`.
- Selected: `border-primary/55 bg-primary/5 ring-1 ring-primary/25`. Unselected:
  `border-border/80 bg-card hover:bg-muted/40`. That change is the only selection indicator.
- The whole tile is one `<button type="button">` with `cursor-pointer`, `aria-pressed={selected}`
  and a visible focus ring, so keyboard and screen-reader users still get the state.
- If another module needs the same pattern, move the tile to `components/shared/` and reuse it.
  Never copy the markup.

## Do NOT

- Put a radio dot or a checkbox inside the tile. It repeats what the tile already shows and adds
  noise.
- Build the pattern as a `RadioGroup` with cards used as labels.

## Two options are never a combo

**A choice between two options never goes in a `Select`/combo.** It is two tiles (`OptionTile`,
`components/shared/option-tile.tsx`, like Single dealer / Multiple dealers in Add Expense) or, for a plain on/off,
a switch. The default option starts selected.

If the choice decides which fields are filled (Type Regular / Piecework in Add Expense), **once data of one option
was entered, the other tile is disabled**, with one line under the tiles that says what to clear to change it
(`expenseKindLocked`, `lib/expenses/expense-piecework.ts`).

**Control:** `lib/ui-guards.test.ts` fails on a `<SelectContent>` with exactly two fixed `<SelectItem>`. The four that
were already there are listed in the test as pending, with the task `ui-dos-opciones-a-tarjetas` in
`plans/plan-general/PLAN-GENERAL.md`; when one of those screens is touched, it goes to tiles and leaves the list.

*(Ignacio, 22/11/2026: T5 of plan-reunion-juan-18-11 shipped «Type» as a two-option Select; «nunca hagas un combo de
dos opciones… esas card que quedan lindas».)*

## When NOT to use tiles

- **Filters (the Filters panel of a list) never use tiles.** A short filter with 2–4 fixed options
  (All / Regular / Piecework in Expenses List, Pending / Corrected in Punch Report) is the compact
  `SegmentedControl` (`components/shared/segmented-control.tsx`: grey track, the chosen option white with a shadow
  and the company color; hover only darkens the text), on one line with its label, sharing the row with other small
  controls (Show deleted). Tiles take the whole width for a few words. Never the raw `ToggleGroup variant="outline"`:
  its padding is too tight and its hover paints the same color as the chosen option. *(Ignacio, 22/11/2026: «mirá todo
  el espacio que ocupa… jamás ocupar todo ese espacio sin necesidad»; «quedó mal el padding».)*
- More than ~5 options, or options with no description → `Select` / `SearchableCombobox`.
- On/off or multi-select options → `BillingToggleRow` / checkboxes.

## Example

```tsx
// ✅ The tile is the selection
<div className="grid gap-1.5 sm:grid-cols-3">
  <BillingOptionTile
    selected={kind === 'invoice'}
    onSelect={() => setKind('invoice')}
    title={t('invoices.bulkExportTypeInvoice')}
    description={t('invoices.bulkExportTypeInvoiceHint')}
    icon={List}
  />
</div>

// ❌ Radio dot inside the tile
<button type="button" className="… rounded-lg border …">
  <span className="size-4 rounded-full border …" />
  …
</button>
```
